          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A comma double category is a generalization of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+categories"} double categories
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+double+categories"} virtual double categories
          %p
            There are two apparently-maximal levels of generality of this construction, which intersect nontrivially but do not coincide. The first is an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+object"} comma object
            in the 2-category of virtual double categories (which includes as a full sub-2-category the 2-category of pseudo double categories and
            %em lax
            functors), which produces another virtual double category. The second is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colax%2Flax+comma+object"} colax/lax comma object
            relative to the 2-monad whose algebras are pseudo double categories. The two constructions intersect in the case of the comma object of a pseudo double functor over a lax one, between pseudo double categories.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#for_virtual_double_categories For virtual double categories
          %p
            For virtual double categories, the comma virtual double category has the universal property of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+object"} comma object
            in the 2-category of virtual double categories, functors and vertical transformations.
          %p
            Explicitly, it is constructed as follows. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C, D, E
            be virtual double categories and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : C \to E, G : D \to E
            be functors of virtual double categories. The comma double category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F / G
            is defined as
          %ol
            %li
              Its vertical category is the ordinary comma category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_v/G_v
              of the vertical components of the functors. We write an object as
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi F
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi G
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A : F A_C \to G A_D
            %li
              A horizontal arrow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              consists of horizontal arrows
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi C
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_C : A_C \to B_C
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi D
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_D : A_D \to B_D
              and a 2-cell in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F R_C
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G R_D
              along
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
            %li
              A 2-cell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_1,\ldots,R_n
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              consists of a pair of 2-cells
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi C
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi nC
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_C : (R_{1C},\ldots,R_{nC}) \to S_C
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi D
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi nD
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_D : (R_{1D},\ldots,R_{nD}) \to S_D
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi S
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(\alpha_D) \circ (R_1,\ldots,R_n) = S \circ F(\alpha_C)
          %h3#the_oplaxlax_case The oplax/lax case
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo ⇉
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K = Cat^{\rightrightarrows}
            be the 2-category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graphs"} directed graphs
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-monad"} 2-monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            whose algebras are (pseudo) double categories, and whose lax and colax morphisms are lax and colax double functors respectively. The oplax/lax comma double category is then an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oplax%2Flax+comma+object"} oplax/lax comma object
            for this 2-monad.
          %h2#structures_on_a_comma_virtual_double_category Structures on a comma virtual double category
          %p
            Next, we consider what properties are required of the input data (in the virtual case) to determine that a comma virtual double category has units and composites. An analogy with the double category case gives some guidance. Since functors of virtual double categories correspond to
            %em lax
            functors of double categories, we don’t have any requirements for the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            on top of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            having composites or units. On the other hand, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            to be “oplax”, we require that it be normal for units or furthermore strong for composites.
          .un_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              If
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              have units and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is a normal functor, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F / G
              has units.
          .un_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              If
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              have composites and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is a strong functor, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F / G
              has composites.
          %p Next, the comma has restrictions whenever the constituent categories do and the functors preserve them.
          .un_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              If
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              have restrictions and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F,G
              preserve them, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F / G
              has restrictions.
          %p In practice this proof burden can be reduced if we are interested in virtual equipments (i.e. having units and restrictions) because in that case restrictions are automatically preserved. We summarize this as follows:
          .un_prop
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C, D
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipments"} virtual equipments
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is a normal functor, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F/G
              is a virtual equipment.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                A monad
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                in the horizontal bicategory of a double category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is equivalent to a lax functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T : 1 \to C
                from the terminal double category. In this case we might call
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_C / T
                the
                = succeed "." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    slice double category
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/slice+double+category"} ?
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategories"} generalized multicategories
                can be constructed using a slice category when the monad
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+monad"} polynomial monad
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                be the double category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+functors"} polynomial functors
                in some locally cartesian closed category
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                can be identified with a horizontal monad in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_C/T
                is then equivalent to the “horizontal Kleisli category” presented in Cruttwell-Shulman;
                = succeed "-multicategories" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %li
              %p
                The double category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decorated+cospans"} decorated cospans
                is naturally constructed as a comma double category. Given a symmetric lax monoidal functor
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo :
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi D
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : (C,+) \to (D,\otimes)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ′
                      %mo :
                      %mi Cospan
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi BD
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F' : Cospan(C) \to BD
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi BD
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BD
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                into a double category whose horizontal category is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                and vertical category is the terminal category. Then there is a colax (pseudo even) double functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi BD
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} * : 1 \to BD
                that picks out the unique object of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi BD
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BD
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi F
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} */F'
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset-valued+sets"} poset-valued sets
                given by an endofunctor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Rel
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel
                and a poset
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                can be viewed as the comma double category from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                to
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Rel
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                is a colax endofunctor of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Rel
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel
                %em horizontal
                morphisms in the resulting comma double category.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dialectica+construction"} Dialectica construction
                associated to an internal poset
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
                in a monoidal category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                with pullbacks can be obtained as a comma double category. Let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Span
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Span(C)
                be the double category whose horizontal morphisms are spans in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\times C^{op}
                as a double category in the horizontal direction, and let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi Span
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C\times C^{op} \to Span(C)
                be the colax functor defined on objects by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,B) \mapsto A\otimes B
                and taking the pair
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
